{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795042000000-engineer-p1b-stage1-codex-gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1794976827310-architect-gambit-ffi-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795040000000-reviewer-p1a-confirm-codex.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md; do printf '\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; if test -f \"$f\"; then wc -l \"$f\"; sed -n '\"'1,160p' \\\"\"'$f\"; fi; done'","aggregated_output":"\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795042000000-engineer-p1b-stage1-codex-gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n     177 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795042000000-engineer-p1b-stage1-codex-gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-19 04:23:45 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-19 11:23:45 ICT (+07)\nCoding-Agent: Codex (implementation continuation)\nSession-ID: 01a1f1f9-2d2b-71a2-b88a-1675b81dd975\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-19 04:03:03 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-19 11:03:03 ICT (+07)\n\n| Capability | Implementation / qualification | Still refused or unqualified |\n|---|---|---|\n| Native bridge | Ahead-of-time typed generated shims; pinned source/runtime inputs; build/output digests recorded | Other runtime configurations/platforms |\n| Loader and resolution | Scoped RTLD_NOW/LOCAL handles; declaration order; address-based duplicate detection; global fallback; ABI/public/load-failure gates | Pending module-native discovery and full dependency loader integration |\n| Memory | Allocation/free, scalar typed access/sizes, zeroing, u8vector bulk copies, public string-byte copies; ABI/public/G1 witnesses | Jolt-array moves and object pinning |\n| Ordinary calls | Finite registered fixed/declared-tail shapes, proper variadic ABI identity, exact widths, nullable strings, float/bool conversion; ABI/public gates | Unknown signatures, string arguments, pointer-call form, aggregates, runtime signature construction |\n| Native error | Invocation-local two-value adapter return, public jolt vector; immediate C capture, generated-shim clobber test, wrong-order control, preserving success and post-capture controls | Blocking+errno; native concurrency/reentrant callback qualification |\n| Collection | Real ##gc and unreachable weak-key clearance in gambitcheck | Collection during C calls / GC-safe blocking |\n| Compiler routing | Explicit :gambit primitive prefix; eval-visible adapter constructors; regenerated seeds; Q1/Q2 public refusal tests | AOT/FASL/subprocess parity |\n| Callbacks | All constructors deliberately refuse; public and adapter refusal witnesses | Dynamic closures, foreign pthreads including serialized mode, yielding/migration/exception escape |\n| Platforms / consumers | Provisioned single-threaded Gambit 4.9.8 on macOS arm64 only | Linux/MT/Web; P2/P3 consumer parity remains Chez-only |\n\nGate summary: PASS — gambitffi, gambitffi-abi, gambitffi-errno-check,\ngambitffi-load-failure-smoke, gambitcheck, gambitkernel, gambiteval,\ngambitseedcheck, gambitgencheck, gambittwins and adaptercheck. Additional Chez\nbinding/width gates pass. The additional Chez native-error suite is 39/41 on\nboth changed and untouched-base compiler seeds (same two socket-consumer rows).\n\n## State and delivery\n\nBase: a6852ef8 on gambit-ffi-p1b. Implementation commit:\nb717f57d93510aac027eeee157c34e3f4c432cbd. Nothing pushed. Session identity was\ncaptured from CODEX_THREAD_ID / CODEX_SESSION_ID in the running environment.\n\nIntended promotion path:\n`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795042000000-engineer-p1b-stage1-codex-gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`.\nThat destination is outside this session's writable roots and was designated\nread-only by the continuation request. This local file and the final response\nsupply the findings for orchestrator promotion; datomworld was not modified.\n\nThe inherited diff was audited and corrected, not accepted as qualification.\nRemoved scratch float experiments and replaced the hand-maintained shim list\nwith a generated registry. Fixed fixed/variadic ABI conflation, wrong Chez type\ntokens, lost scoped blocking/varargs conventions, unspecified errno read order,\ncell cleanup, and an accidental host/bridge free-name collision. Replaced\nmalformed tests, double frees and freeing an integer result. Corrected the gsc\noptions and added an assertion that errno instrumentation actually compiled.\n\n## Runtime and bootstrap\n\nAbsolute runtime prefix:\n`/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8`.\nRuntime header version is 409008 and the installed driver/header select the\nsingle-threaded configuration. Host: Darwin arm64. Apple clang 21.0.0\n(clang-2100.4.1.1). No Gambit runtime source/build was modified.\n\n`host/gambit/ffi/TOOLCHAIN.sha256` records gsi/gsc, gambuild-C, gambit.h and the\nthree runtime/compiler static libraries. `DIGESTS.txt` pins nine source,\ngenerated and toolchain-manifest inputs. Generated shims must match their\nmanifest on every build. FFI prerequisites force rebuilding artifacts, so a\nfresh timestamp on an ambient .o1 cannot satisfy a gate. The missing dependency\nfixture is built from pinned C sources and its dependency is removed before\nloading. Output hashes and generated-C hashes are in evidence/; binary\nbyte-identity across linkers/machines is not claimed.\n\nEvery gate is externally watchdogged. The watchdog kills the entire process\ngroup and returns 124. Missing tools or a missing bridge cannot produce an FFI\nPASS. Digest corruption and deadline rejection were independently exercised.\n\n## Public-path proof and deviations\n\n`gambitffi-public.ss` uses the fork's actual boot + cross-minted seed and\n`jolt-compile-eval`, explicitly asserting backend target :gambit. Its 31 jolt\nsource checks exercise the host-owned jolt.ffi functions and compiler __cfn\nspecial form, plus actual catchable refusals. The ABI target and gambitcheck\nboth run it. This is the brief's permitted compiler/eval gate path: the build\nCLI is Chez-hosted; no Gambit `bin/jolt build`, AOT, complete stdlib namespace\nloading or dependency-native discovery is claimed. No Chez subprocess executes\nthese Gambit public witnesses.\n\nGambit eval cannot see unit-local sa-* macros. Rather than emit unbound names,\nthe :gambit emitter calls the host's public error boundary, then the adapter's\nregistered-shape constructor with literal type data. Same-unit Scheme callers\nstill have the sa-* syntaxes. All C FFI forms are compiled ahead of time.\n\nThe checked-in JSON manifest is an explicit projection of inventory shapes:\n20 unique ordinary ABI identities including S32, with S19/S20 shared, plus 13\nscalar identity fixtures. It rejects all omitted modes and unknown shapes.\nThere is no libffi dependency or unrestricted runtime ABI engine.\n\nErrno is saved immediately after the typed target call, before conversion or\ncleanup. Each invocation owns a malloc'd cell, allocated before calling C and\nfreed with dynamic-wind even on conversion failure. Ordered let* ensures the\ncell is read after the call. The test build uses exactly the generated capture\nbodies, adding errno=4242 after saving; it asserts that flag is enabled. The\nwrong-order control calls the same target but saves after its clobber and must\nsee 4242; the real shim must see EACCES. Success returning errno 7777,\npreset-and-preserving C-frame control, post-capture overwrite and 1000\nalternating pairs also pass. Alternating stress is not advertised as native\nconcurrency or nesting qualification.\n\nThe original proposal to preset errno in a separate Scheme call was discarded:\nallocation/conversion before the next call can affect errno. Targets own their\nfailure/success errnos; the preserving-success preset and call share a C frame.\n\n## Q1–Q6\n\n- Q1: :ffi-layout and named by-value forms refuse before Chez ftype emission;\n  public jolt catches UnsupportedOperationException. No unbound fallback.\n- Q2: scoped and global named calls use the finite registry, preserve variadic\n  boundaries and blocking refusals; public unknown-shape and missing-symbol\n  tests assert messages, so an unrelated unbound error cannot pass.\n- Q3: worker destruction's unmarked pthread_join remains a P3 consumer issue.\n  S18's nonblocking fixture qualifies only its ABI. Do not enable parser\n  workers on this evidence; re-mark or separately qualify the consumer wait.\n- Q4: explicit :gambit {:unsafe-prefix \"\"} entry implemented and reminted.\n- Q5: both callable syntaxes added to CONTRACT.txt, with refusal assertions.\n- Q6: unmarked SSL waits remain unqualified; no libssl or HTTP consumer parity\n  is claimed. Address their marks in the consumer lane before adoption.\n\nThe architecture/trampoline constraint and all C4 must-not-assert restrictions\nremain binding. There is no claim of universal foreign-entry impossibility,\nMT teardown corruption, or a precise post-return GC delay.\n\n## Commands and verbatim verdicts\n\nRequired run (exit 0, watchdog 300s):\n\n```sh\nsh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 300 make gambitffi gambitcheck gambitkernel gambiteval GAMBIT_PREFIX=/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8\n```\n\n```text\ngambitffi-abi: 78 check(s), 0 failure(s)\ngambitffi-abi: PASS\ngambitffi-public: 31 check(s), 0 failure(s)\ngambitffi-public: PASS — :gambit compiler + native gsi\ngambitffi-errno-check: 9 check(s), 0 failure(s)\ngambitffi-errno-check: PASS\ngambitffi-load-failure-smoke: 14 check(s), 0 failure(s)\ngambitffi-load-failure-smoke: PASS\ngambitffi: PASS — all stage-1 qualified-tier gates green\ngambitcheck: 0 failure(s)\ngambitcheck: PASS — gambit adapter + shims verified on native gsi\ngambitffi-public: 31 check(s), 0 failure(s)\ngambitffi-public: PASS — :gambit compiler + native gsi\nkernel-test: 0 failure(s)\nkernel-test: PASS — booted kernel + natives verified on native gsi\neval-test: 0 failure(s)\neval-test: PASS — jolt compiles and evaluates on native gsi\n```\n\nSeeds reminted with the provisioned Chez bin/scheme using host/chez/remint.sh;\nconverged after two final passes, zero skipped forms, Gambit grep verification\npassed. make gambitseedcheck / gambitgencheck / gambittwins (watchdog 180s) and\nadaptercheck (30s) passed. Chez ffi-binding-test.ss (90s): 74/74; widths shell\ngate (120s): 86/86.\n\nAdditional Chez native-error shell gate (120s): 39/41. A comparison loading\nboth untouched a6852ef8 seed files, forcing the source boot and using the same\nnative helper, reproduces exactly:\n\n```text\n\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1794976827310-architect-gambit-ffi-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n     283 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1794976827310-architect-gambit-ffi-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-18 04:44:48 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-17 23:44:48 EST (-0500)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a1ecf5-dc51-7210-8e9e-79f452f849ae\n\n\nThe supplied checkout has no `build/tools/jolt`, and its toolchain declaration still has an empty patch list. I’ll look for an existing local clone without bootstrapping or fetching. The native sources also show that `jolt-file` is the POSIX file wrapper; the ANTLR wrapper is a separate component that needs its own P3 scope.\n\nI found a clean sibling jolt clone at the exact pinned SHA, so I can inspect the contracts and gates while keeping that source provenance explicit. Two design constraints are clear: Gambit’s boot excludes the FFI runtime, and jolt constructs some foreign procedures from runtime signatures. A `c-lambda` macro mapping alone would not cover the existing API.\n\nThe critical GC gate needs strengthening: Gambit currently implements `sa-gc-collect` as a no-op, so a quick return from `gc-full!` cannot prove that collection completed during a native call. P1 must demonstrate both real collection and progress by another Scheme thread. I also found direct Chez `foreign-procedure` emission in scoped-symbol and varargs paths that must be routed through the adapter.\n\nI haven’t found Gambit 4.9.8 headers or runtime source locally. I’ll distinguish the feasible errno shim design from the release/claim and foreign-thread entry mechanisms that remain unverified, rather than certify the full tier from memory.\n\nThe plan will gate each FFI capability separately, retaining refusal tests for every unsupported operation. P2 and P3 will require explicit Gambit launchers: the current qualification scripts hardcode the Chez launcher. I’ll also call out two delivery prerequisites: `qualify:jolt-upgrade` is documented but absent from `bb.edn`, and the patch verifier needs final-tree validation before successive patches can safely touch the same files.\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-18 04:44:48 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-17 23:44:48 EST (-0500)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a1ecf5-dc51-7210-8e9e-79f452f849ae\n\nIntended artifact: `collab/jolt/1794976827310-architect-gambit-ffi-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`\n\nThe plan follows. **No file was written:** the task explicitly prohibits modifications, and this session’s filesystem is read-only. No builds, gates, tests, or network requests were run.\n\n**Evidence and scope.** This checkout, at `65155a9dd2b5b165b1c1a10009f0d944dbba842c`, has no `build/tools/jolt`. I inspected the existing sibling `/home/sto/ci/datomworld-j6ev/build/tools/jolt`, whose HEAD is the declared pin `b389f1a7fb553f63a3494d71f8fe3251790bf4d3` and whose working tree is clean.\n\nThe supplied checkout also lacks a Gambit entry in `mise.toml`. I found no local Gambit 4.9.8 headers or runtime source. Consequently, the task’s provisioning and historical gate results are supplied context, not independently verified findings.\n\nSeveral source findings materially affect the plan:\n\n- `TARGET-CONTRACT.md` requires atomic native-error capture and GC-safe blocking independently.\n- `host/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:321–459` refuses FFI; its `sa-gc-collect` at line 81 is a no-op.\n- `backend_scheme.clj:297` contains only the Chez primitive-table entry. Gambit currently obtains checked operations through the missing-entry fallback.\n- Scoped symbol calls still emit raw Chez `foreign-procedure` at `backend_scheme.clj:2134`; runtime varargs construction does likewise in `host/chez/java/ffi.ss:1294`.\n- Gambit’s boot omits the FFI runtime. Adapter changes alone cannot enable `jolt.ffi`.\n- ANTLR lives in `native/jolt-parser`, not `native/jolt-file`. The latter is the POSIX wrapper supporting J4a–J4d.\n- `qualify:jolt-upgrade` is described as proposed in design §6.4 and is absent from `bb.edn`.\n\n**1. Feasibility ruling**\n\n**Proceed with a bounded P1 feasibility and adapter slice. Do not yet promise complete Chez-equivalent FFI on Gambit 4.9.8.** Atomic errno capture has a concrete implementation path. Generic GC-safe blocking and foreign-thread callbacks need a runtime-source proof and executable witnesses before their refusal rows change.\n\n| Contract surface | Ruling |\n|---|---|\n| Scalar calls, native memory, `dlopen`/symbol lookup | Feasible through a compiled native bridge exposed with Gambit `c-lambda`, with declarations/helpers supplied through `c-declare`. |\n| Atomic C result plus errno | Feasible through typed C wrappers that save errno immediately after the target returns, before conversion, cleanup, allocation, or runtime re-entry. |\n| Blocking calls allowing other threads’ GC | Plain `c-lambda` is insufficient evidence. Enable only after proving the exact 4.9.8 runtime transition and independent Scheme-thread progress. |\n| Same-thread Scheme callbacks | `c-define` supplies an entry mechanism, but dynamic closure identity, rooting, lifetime, exceptions, and re-entry still require implementation. |\n| Callbacks from an unrelated C thread | Unqualified. Requires verified attachment/claim, Scheme entry, and release/detachment rules for the exact runtime build. |\n| Arbitrary runtime signatures | Not provided merely by expanding to compile-time `c-lambda`. Needs a runtime ABI engine or explicitly bounded compiled signature support. |\n| Native compilation, FASL, subprocess | Separate capabilities; remain refused unless independently implemented and qualified. |\n\nThere is an important evidence limit: **I cannot name a verified 4.9.8 release/claim API or declare these rows impossible on every 4.9.8 configuration from the available source.** Supplying exact function names or signatures here would be speculation. This is an unresolved P1 prerequisite, not an assertion that later Gambit APIs exist in 4.9.8.\n\nThe implementer must inspect the exact provisioned 4.9.8 source and record:\n\n1. Runtime build configuration and threading model.\n2. Exact declarations and implementations of any leave/re-enter or release/claim mechanism.\n3. Whether a released foreign frame permits collection and whether another Scheme thread can actually run.\n4. Rules for a previously unknown pthread entering Scheme.\n5. Root and processor-state obligations across those transitions.\n\nNeither `___ATOMIC` nor a C atomic variable establishes these properties. Object-reference release is also distinct from releasing runtime execution ownership. Do not translate Chez `__collect_safe` to a similarly named Gambit mechanism without proving its semantics.\n\nA single processor blocked inside C cannot run another green thread merely because its arguments are rooted. Conversely, enabling multiple native execution contexts does not establish that a foreign frame participates correctly in collection.\n\n**Fallback ruling:** permit explicitly documented FFI sub-capabilities. If blocking or foreign-thread entry cannot be qualified on the pinned runtime, those constructors continue to raise message-carrying unsupported errors. The basic memory/loader/call tier need not remain entirely disabled. Update `CONTRACT.txt` and `TARGET-CONTRACT.md` to describe this partial mode; do not silently redefine “FFI supported” to mean full support.\n\nA worker pool is **not** a transparent generic fallback. Moving calls changes native-thread identity, TLS, callback context, and potentially library behavior. A separately qualified worker interface for specific libraries is possible, but cannot masquerade as unrestricted `sa-foreign-procedure-blocking`.\n\n**2. Adapter and compiler design**\n\nAdd an explicit `:gambit` entry with `:unsafe-prefix \"\"` to `target-prims`. Keep generated code expressed through the adapter boundary; Gambit C forms belong in target-owned bridge compilation units.\n\nP1 must cover all foreign-call construction paths:\n\n- Ordinary and blocking `sa-foreign-procedure`.\n- `sa-foreign-procedure-native-error`, including combinations with supported conventions.\n- Both callable forms.\n- Runtime signature construction.\n- Scoped-address calls.\n- Varargs specialization.\n\nReplace the raw Chez scoped-address emission and runtime `eval` of `foreign-procedure` with adapter operations. Audit aggregate/ftype emission separately. Unsupported ABI forms must raise deliberately, rather than reach an unbound Chez identifier.\n\nThe contract’s “four syntaxes” wording is now incomplete: native-error capture adds another entry. Inventory actual bindings and call sites rather than relying on that count.\n\n**Compilation boundary.** Gambit’s C FFI forms are compiled facilities. An interpreted REPL must not depend on evaluating new `c-lambda` forms successfully. Build a native bridge ahead of time; Scheme adapter macros expand to calls into that bridge. Loading or linking this bridge does not require implementing jolt’s general AOT capability.\n\nFor the first implementation, use **typed, generated C shims per call shape**, shared across symbols with identical ABI shapes. Each accepts the resolved function address. Signature identity includes argument/result types, calling convention, variadic boundary, and relevant ABI layout.\n\nThis gives auditable errno capture and avoids a hand-written universal C ABI dispatcher. It also imposes an explicit limitation: a finite precompiled registry cannot claim arbitrary runtime-signature support. Unregistered shapes must raise. Generate the initial registry from the native consumer inventory and test fixtures.\n\nA later generic engine may use a pinned dependency such as libffi, but requires separate approval of its dependency and ABI surface. In particular, reading errno after `ffi_call` is insufficient unless its intervening return path is proved errno-preserving. Calling a typed shim that saves errno internally avoids that dependency.\n\n**Native-error wrapper.** The essential sequence is:\n\n```c\nresult_type result = target(arg0, arg1);\nint saved_errno = errno;\n/* Only now: store the pair, clean up, or re-enter Scheme. */\n```\n\nUse invocation-local storage, including for nested/reentrant calls. A global “last errno” cell is forbidden; a single TLS cell also fails nested-call ownership.\n\nReturn the two Scheme values expected by the existing lowering. Preserve the target’s errno value on both success and failure; do not normalize success to zero unless the existing contract explicitly requires it. For blocking calls, save errno before reclaiming runtime ownership. Any cleanup that changes errno occurs afterward.\n\n**Blocking transition.** Once the runtime mechanism is verified, the adapter must perform this sequence:\n\n1. Resolve and retain the symbol/library.\n2. Convert arguments and establish ownership while Scheme execution is active.\n3. Ensure C will access only stable native memory or specifically supported rooted representations.\n4. Leave runtime execution through the verified mechanism.\n5. Invoke the target and capture result/errno.\n6. Re-enter the runtime through its required claim mechanism.\n7. Convert results, release temporary resources, and return.\n\nNo Scheme allocation, Scheme-object access, or callback may occur in the released interval without the corresponding entry protocol. Failure and cancellation paths must preserve ownership and reclaim exactly once.\n\n**Memory.** Keep the consumers’ existing copy-in/copy-out design. `dao.posix.file` already documents that caller arrays are not shared with C.\n\nImplement allocation, free, typed access, sizes, and bulk copies with correct widths and pointer representation. Cover `size_t`, signed/unsigned 64-bit values, floating types, null pointers, and byte offsets.\n\nGambit must not inherit the non-moving-collector exemption for `sa-lock-object`. Rooting prevents reclamation; it does not necessarily prevent relocation. For callback objects, a rooted Scheme handle plus a stable C trampoline may satisfy the required callable lifetime. For operations requiring an actual pinned Scheme address, provide a verified pinning mechanism or refuse that operation.\n\nBulk bytevector copies remain short, non-collect-safe operations with no callbacks or allocations while borrowing an address, as the contract requires.\n\n**Loading and resolution.** Preserve the current loader policy:\n\n- Declared libraries use scoped handles and declared-order symbol resolution.\n- Scoped lookup precedes process-global lookup.\n- Missing files differ from present-but-unloadable files.\n- Preserve the loader’s reason, including missing dependencies.\n- Duplicate-symbol detection compares distinct addresses, not handle counts.\n- Handles remain alive while procedures or callable machinery can reference their code.\n\nExpose `sa-load-shared-object`, process-symbol lookup, entry predicates, and addresses through this implementation. Do not replace scoped lookup with global linking merely because that makes `c-lambda` easier.\n\n**Callbacks.** A `c-define` entry alone does not create one independently owned native function pointer for every runtime Scheme closure. Use qualified trampolines/closures with explicit rooting and release, or document a bounded implementation that refuses exhaustion.\n\nSeparate three entry cases: active Scheme-origin calls, re-entry during a released blocking call, and previously unknown C threads. The last two require verified runtime ownership rules before Scheme executes.\n\nPreserve existing callback exception/error handling; do not allow Scheme escapes or C++ exceptions to unwind across an unsupported C frame. Test nested callbacks and callbacks that allocate.\n\nThese callbacks remain host-local runtime machinery. They must not introduce portable yin.vm callbacks or migratable native handles. Application-visible asynchronous effects still enter the stream boundary. A queued event is not an equivalent substitute for a synchronous C callback requiring a return value.\n\n**3. Gate strategy**\n\nMaintain explicit profiles for native FFI and FFI-disabled execution, including Gambit’s JavaScript target.\n\nSplit G1 assertions by individual capability. In the enabled profile, replace each implemented refusal with a behavioral witness using real fixture symbols. In the disabled profile, preserve its refusal. Unimplemented signatures and sub-capabilities retain negative tests in both relevant profiles.\n\nDo not select expectations by attempting an operation and accepting either success or failure. The qualified profile determines the expected result.\n\nRetain native-compilation, FASL, and subprocess refusal rows. A native bridge does not justify flipping them.\n\nProposed targets:\n\n| Target | Required evidence |\n\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795040000000-reviewer-p1a-confirm-codex.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n     171 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795040000000-reviewer-p1a-confirm-codex.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-18 18:59:06 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-19 01:59:06 ICT (+07)\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-19 01:59:06 ICT (+07)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a1ef93-bee4-7c00-9752-4e034b3efcd1\nRole: Review\n\n**The bounded P1b ruling is confirmed. F1–F3 are repaired for the provisioned-build audit. The MT blocked-GC failure also reproduces. However, the MT report overstates its teardown evidence, and its “183µs after return” timing claim needs correction.**\n\nReviewed commits:\n\n- P1a-r2: `6507ec1ed5533504b22955aa20a37af9e1d1e155`.\n- P1a-MT: `1b9b6ed09f966dfe2e5e065938be67ca16f17477`.\n- Gambit: `2228e4d163582733a3a67a5efcb22f8ebe93c02b`.\n\nI ran the r2 suite and generated-C verification, repeated repaired W3 rows, and reran existing MT configuration, blocked-GC, owner-control, and naive serialized-entry binaries. Writes were confined to `p1a/witnesses/build/`. Both worktrees’ tracked files remain unchanged.\n\nPath abbreviations: `W/` = this worktree’s `p1a/witnesses/`; `MT/` = `/Users/sto/workspace/jolt-ffi-mt/p1a-mt/`; `S/` = `/Users/sto/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src/`.\n\n**C1 — Repairs against F1–F3**\n\n**F1 repair — CONFIRMED.**\n\nThe two add entries now specify `(int int)`; the zero-argument phase3 entry specifies `()` (`W/w3-server.scm:56–63,96–97`). Generated C contains two C arguments and `___NARGS 2` for both add entries (`W/build/w3-server.c:197–205,228–233`). `make verify-w3-c` passed, including its phase3 `___NARGS 0` check (`W/Makefile:88–100`).\n\nEvery provisioned-build phase runs the owner control first and aborts on control failure (`W/w3-fthread.c:74–101,267–275`).\n\nMy samples reproduced:\n\n| Repaired W3 row | Reviewer result |\n|---|---|\n| Owner control | PASS: 42, 1072, two GC rounds |\n| Serialized phase1 | 3/3 survived with correct recorded values |\n| Serialized phase1, heartbeat disabled | 3/3 survived |\n| Owner parked inside cfun, phase3 | 3/3 survived |\n| Concurrent phase2 | 3/3 SIGSEGV, exit 139 |\n\nThe serialized workers reached the three `(##gc)` rounds and final result 2 (`W/w3-fthread.c:125–131`; `W/results/w3-phase3.out:10–20`). The original malformed-fixture crash attribution must remain withdrawn.\n\n**F2 repair — CONFIRMED.**\n\nThe actual targets now set their own failure errno (`W/w1-fixture.c:53–55`). Shapes A/B/C save errno immediately after the target and before allocation (`:64–68,81–85,97–101`). Shape E saves before allocation and nested Scheme re-entry (`:120–125`).\n\nThe contrasting controls are effective:\n\n- F saves before the intervening clobber (`:144–149`).\n- G deliberately saves after it (`:163–168`).\n- Scheme checks G’s clobbered result explicitly and exercises F/G in the loops (`W/w1-errno.scm:138–163`).\n\nMy rerun produced **5901 checks, zero failures, negative-control OK** (`W/build/results/w1.out:4–8`). This confirms the repaired standalone capture witness; it does not yet qualify jolt’s future shim generator or blocking-plus-errno path.\n\n**F3 repair — CONFIRMED for the provisioned-build refusal witness.**\n\nB executes and announces readiness before A enters C (`W/w2-blocking.scm:199–217`). B mutates a globally rooted object, performs real GC, and calls the C handshake only after a completed collection (`:111–145`). C records release cause and whether the handshake arrived during its active wait (`W/w2-fixture.c:47–83,91–104`). The qualification verdict derives from observed state (`W/w2-blocking.scm:223–263`).\n\nMy rerun reproduced:\n\n```text\nrelease-cause=deadline\nrounds-during-block=0\nrounds-after=3\nhandshake-called-from-scheme=#t\ncapi-handshake-calls=1\nhandshake-during-block=0\nblocked-ms=6000\n```\n\nEvidence: `W/build/results/w2-qualify.out:22–25`.\n\nOne limitation remains: the control’s “overlap” predicate permits all B collections before A’s first call because it tests only `a-calls-done < 3` (`W/w2-blocking.scm:128–129,182–186`). Treat it as a working-GC control, not proof of collection overlapping C execution. This does not undermine the synchronized negative qualification result.\n\n`make all` and `make verify-w3-c` passed. Build hygiene now preserves tracked results and existing `build/results/` through `clean` (`W/Makefile:239–258`). `VERIFY: PASS` means the expected audit records—including capability failures—were obtained.\n\nThe F5 citation corrections are also present, including the Threads introduction, conversion-temporary and `___AT_END` passages, stack-marker TODO, and corrected synchronization locations (`p1a/AUDIT.md:78–83,115–120,588–599`).\n\n**C2 — Effect of serialized foreign-entry survival**\n\nIt narrows the empirical conclusion materially:\n\n**On this provisioned single-threaded build, a foreign pthread can execute these valid entries, allocate, collect, and return while the owner is parked.** A categorical assertion that foreign entry necessarily crashes is false.\n\nThe qualification boundary remains unchanged. These samples do not establish a documented ownership-transfer protocol, exclusion against every runtime activity, lifecycle safety, nested-entry rules, or portability. Concurrent use of the shared pstate still crashes. The configured single-threaded processor accessor selects the same primordial pstate regardless of calling pthread (`S/include/gambit.h.in:8607–8612`).\n\nP1b may qualify callbacks on the owning native thread under explicit nesting, scheduling, lifetime, and exception restrictions. It must retain refusal for generic unrelated-pthread entry, including an advertised “serialized foreign-thread” mode. Serialized survival is positive experimental evidence, not qualification of that mode.\n\n**C3 — MT experiment and standard of proof**\n\n**The MT blocking verdict is confirmed; the complete five-step qualification is not finished.**\n\n| Experiment requirement | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| Pinned, genuinely SMP-enabled build | Confirmed by configuration/bootstrap records and live behavior |\n| Explicit processor startup | Confirmed: `-:p2`; server sets parallelism 2 |\n| B executing elsewhere while A blocks; real GC before release | Implemented; failure reproduced |\n| Valid entries, owner control, foreign-entry lifecycle investigation | Partly satisfied; attach stress has defects described below |\n| macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 | macOS executed; Linux pending |\n\nThe bootstrap distinction is substantive: regenerating the runtime modules with the SMP prelude is documented at `MT/BUILD-CONFIG.md:69–115`. The current generated scheduler contains the cited SMP implementation, and my W0MT rerun reports two processors, TLS pstate access, and `smp-feature=ENABLED`.\n\nI also compared the ten restored source-tree runtime `.c` files’ actual Git object hashes against the pin; all match. This independently checks restoration after the disclosed regeneration incident.\n\n**The decisive blocked-GC result reproduces.**\n\nIn my fresh MT run:\n\n- A announces entry on processor 1.\n- B observes A inside C and requests `(##gc)` on processor 0.\n- C returns after its approximately six-second deadline.\n- B’s first GC-round completion and release handshake occur afterward.\n\nEvidence: `W/build/review-mt/w2mt-blocked.out:7–16,25–29`. The committed record shows the same sequence (`MT/witnesses/results/w2mt-blocked.out:8–17,26–30`).\n\nThe synchronization source supports the mechanism: `___garbage_collect` calls `on_all_processors`; that interrupts participating processors and enters the barrier (`S/lib/setup.c:1138–1149,1182–1196`). Additional Scheme progress does not make the blocked processor participate.\n\n**Foreign-entry evidence supports refusal, with narrower conclusions.**\n\nI reproduced the MT owner control PASS and naive serialized-entry SIGSEGV. Valid signatures are present (`MT/witnesses/w3mt-server.scm:16–23`); the committed concurrent naive run also segfaults (`MT/witnesses/results/w3mt-p2.out:4–6`).\n\nThe exploratory bridge initializes slot 2 while leaving `processor_count=2`, then binds TLS (`MT/witnesses/w3mt-main.c:194–229`). That omission is independently visible in source: VM interrupts cover only counted processors (`S/lib/setup.c:95–106`). TLS binding plus successful arithmetic is not a complete attachment protocol.\n\nTwo qualifications are necessary:\n\n- **New P2 finding — teardown corruption is NOT CONFIRMED.** `keep_attached` is documented and passed as true, but never honored. The helper unconditionally clears TLS and cleans up the pstate (`MT/witnesses/w3mt-main.c:188–192,275–286`). Its caller then invokes Scheme in the purported “while attached” loop (`:325–331`). The recorded crash follows that cleanup (`MT/witnesses/results/w3mt-bridge-concurrent.out:12–15`). This is an invalid post-detachment entry, not an isolated demonstration that teardown corrupted a correctly attached runtime.\n- **P2 evidence limitation — the bridge GC timeouts do not isolate membership as their sole cause.** In the serialized bridge and reattach tests, the owner is parked in `pthread_join` (`MT/witnesses/w3mt-main.c:434–440,470–472`). That counted processor cannot service GC synchronization either. The recorded 120/180-second timeouts establish failure of these attempted sequences; they do not prove that every possible attachment sequence must deadlock. Repeated attachment and later lifecycle checks were never reached.\n\n**New P3 finding — “collection completed 183µs after C’s return” is NOT CONFIRMED.** The 183µs value is a GC-report duration, not a measured return-to-completion interval (`MT/witnesses/results/w2mt-blocked.out:13–17`). GC timing begins inside the collector (`S/lib/mem.c:6954–6961`). The record establishes completion after C returns and an approximately six-second request-to-round-completion wait. It does not establish that precise 183µs lag.\n\nThus, **MT-VERDICT’s operational conclusion is correct**: this tested configuration fails the blocking contract, and foreign-thread entry remains unqualified. Its teardown attribution and universal attachment-impossibility wording need amendment. The startup/heap crashes remain configuration-specific observations, not established root-cause diagnoses.\n\n**C4 — Final track verdict under astra’s criteria**\n\nYes: the standing ruling is **PARTIAL-GO for bounded P1b work, conditional on qualification of each implemented capability; blocking and foreign-thread rows retain honest refusal; P2/P3 remain Chez-only.**\n\nAstra §5 states verbatim:\n\n> **Partial GO:** memory, loading, ordinary calls, and errno may land independently when qualified. Retain blocking/callback refusals and describe the result as partial FFI. This does **not** authorize P2/P3 parity claims.\n\nIts NO-GO criteria are:\n\n> **NO-GO for P2/P3:** collection cannot complete while C is blocked; unrelated C-thread entry cannot be made safe where required; exact runtime ownership rules remain unknown; or the solution depends on silently moving generic calls to another native thread.\n\nThe blocking failure independently sustains NO-GO on both tested configurations. No universal foreign-entry impossibility proof is required to reach that ruling.\n\nThe next-path language remains:\n\n> If 4.9.8 cannot meet the necessary runtime transitions, the next decision is an explicit Gambit upgrade or runtime patch through the upgrade gate. The fallback remains honest refusal, with Chez retaining the affected capability.\n\nP1b must not assert:\n\n- GC-safe blocking or blocking-plus-errno qualification.\n- Generic foreign-pthread callbacks, including serialized entry.\n- Arbitrary dynamic closure-backed callables; F6’s architecture/trampoline constraint remains.\n- Unrestricted yielding, migration, exception escape, or nested callback behavior.\n- Qualified memory, loader, ABI, or public jolt behavior solely from these standalone fixtures.\n- Linux qualification, full FFI parity, or qualification of AOT/FASL/subprocess capabilities.\n\nQualified callable modes can be included only with their own public-path behavioral gates and explicit restrictions.\n\n**C5 — Readiness for a P1b brief**\n\n\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md\n     313 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/yin.repl.md\n# yin.repl: Quick Start & User Guide\n\n`yin.repl` is the primary user interface into datom.world. It is a cross-host\nshell (JVM, Node, Dart, and Jolt) that evaluates code on `yin.vm`, serves and\nconnects to remote shells over WebSockets, and composes with `dao.jing.dht` to\npublish and load modules by signed name over a distributed hash table (DHT).\nThe same CLI, flags and index-store schemes work on every host; only the\nlaunch command differs (section 1.1).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Quick Start: Launching the REPL\n\n### Launch on each host\n\nThe engine, flags and `--index-store` schemes are identical on all four hosts;\neach host differs only in how the shell starts.\n\n**JVM** (the reference host; every flag is exercised here first):\n\n```bash\nclj -M:clj-yin-repl\n```\n\n**Node** (a self-contained `node-script` build; the first build needs the\ngenerated JS parser, which `bb build:yin-repl-node` produces):\n\n```bash\nbb build:yin-repl-node     # compiles the shadow-cljs :yin-repl build\nnode target/yin-repl.js --index-store mem\n```\n\n**Dart** (ClojureDart compiles the shell to a Dart script):\n\n```bash\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd-yin-repl-build    # emits build/yin-repl/yin_repl.dart\ndart run build/yin-repl/yin_repl.dart --index-store mem\n```\n\n**Jolt** (the native Chez host; the bootstrap materializes the pinned jolt and\nits stdlib — run it once per checkout, then always launch through\n`build/tools/jolt-env.sh` so `$JOLT_HOME/bin` leads PATH; a system/Homebrew\njolt lacks the J6-era stdlib modules):\n\n```bash\nbb bootstrap:jolt\n. build/tools/jolt-env.sh\nexport PATH=\"$JOLT_HOME/bin:$PATH\"\njolt -Srepro -M:jolt:jolt-yin-repl --index-store mem\n```\n\nPer-host store status for `--index-store`: `mem` everywhere; `file:<dir>` on\nJVM, Node, Dart and Jolt (J4+); `dht:<dir>` on JVM, Node, Dart, and on Jolt\nsince the J7 slices 1-2 (2026-11-15; the sections 6-7 process/interop gate of\nyin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §10.1 remains open). `--help` works on every\nhost.\n\n### Local In-Memory REPL\nStart an interactive prompt on the JVM with an in-memory index store:\n\n```bash\nclj -M:clj-yin-repl\n```\n\n> **Note for fresh clones:** `yin.repl` loads the generated ANTLR Python3 parser on startup. Run `bb gen:antlr` (or `clj -M:antlr-gen` for JVM) once before starting the REPL or running tests.\n\n### Durable Local Store\nTo persist definitions and evaluated code across restarts, pass a `file:` directory store:\n\n```bash\nclj -M:clj-yin-repl --index-store file:/tmp/my-repl-store\n```\n\n### Client / Server over WebSockets\nStart a headless or interactive server listening on a WebSocket port:\n\n```bash\nclj -M:clj-yin-repl --port 8080 --headless\n```\n\nFrom another REPL prompt, attach to the running server:\n\n```clojure\nyin> (connect \"daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl\")\nAttaching to daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl\nConnected to daostream:ws://localhost:8080/repl\n```\n\n- An absent URL path defaults to `/repl`; an explicit `/` remains `/`.\n- The wire codec defaults to CBOR. Use `--codec <cbor|transit>` on the server, and `(connect url :codec :transit)` or `:cbor` at the prompt. Both ends must match.\n- `--port` listens on all interfaces (`localhost` and the machine's IP) with **no authentication**—run it only on a trusted network. There is no `--host` flag.\n\n### Other Hosts (Node & Dart)\n`(host)` prints the runtime the shell runs on (`Host: JVM 21.0.5`, `Host: Node v25.6.1`, `Host: Dart`).\n\n- **ClojureScript (`cljs` on Node):**\n  ```bash\n  clj -M:cljs-yin-repl --port 8080 --headless\n  # Or compile to a standalone Node script:\n  npx shadow-cljs release yin-repl\n  node target/yin-repl.js --port 8080 --headless\n  ```\n\n- **ClojureDart (`cljd`):**\n  ```bash\n  clj -M:clojuredart:cljd-yin-repl-build compile\n  dart run bin/yin_repl_main.dart --port 8080 --headless\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Working at the Prompt\n\n### Switching Language Frontends\nThe REPL ships with three frontends on every host: `:yang/clojure` (default), `:yang/php`, and `:yang/python` (ANTLR multi-target parser, revision f4). Switch languages using `(lang <id>)` with a qualified keyword:\n\n```clojure\nyin> (lang :yang/python)\n\"Switched to :yang/python\"\nyin> print(\"hello\")\nhello\n```\n\n- Selection requires a qualified id (`(lang :yang/python)` or `[id revision]`). Unqualified keywords like `(lang :python)` return `Error: unavailable-parser: ...`.\n- The Python frontend displays printed output followed by the value of the last top-level bare expression statement (rendered as Python `repr`; assignments or `None` display nothing). Frontend state is preserved across submissions and cleared on `(reset)` or `(vm ...)`.\n  - *Known edges:* Bare expressions inside compound statements (`if`/`for`) do not display; values captured before a later statement raises print after the error text; string `repr` escapes only code units `< 32` and `127`.\n\n### Switching Evaluator VMs\n`(vm :type)` selects one of four evaluators:\n- `:semantic` (default) — linear semantic VM\n- `:ast-walker` — tree walker\n- `:stack` — de Bruijn stack kernel\n- `:register` — de Bruijn register kernel\n\nSwitching rebuilds the session and clears value history.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Distributed Swarm Quick Start (DHT)\n\nWith `--index-store dht:<dir>` (or the `dht` subcommands), the REPL composes a `dao.space.dht` node over a durable, exclusively locked directory store.\n\nA publication is acknowledged once at least two peers hold it (`ack-peers >= 2`). This walkthrough starts two storing peers (**B**, **B2**), one publisher (**A**), and one reader (**C**) on loopback:\n\n1. **Start two storing peers (B and B2)** in separate terminals. They hold blobs while A publishes and need no key:\n   ```bash\n   clj -M:clj-yin-repl dht serve --name b  --listen 4002 --peer localhost:4001\n   clj -M:clj-yin-repl dht serve --name b2 --listen 4003 --peer localhost:4001\n   ```\n   Wait for `dht: node ... listening on 127.0.0.1:4002; peers: 127.0.0.1:4001; fetch-only` (and `4003`).\n\n2. **Start the publisher (A)** in a third terminal:\n   ```bash\n   clj -M:clj-yin-repl dht init --name a --listen 4001 \\\n       --peer localhost:4002 --peer localhost:4003\n   ```\n   The first run writes an owner-only Ed25519 key to `~/.yin/a.key` and prints its principal (`ed25519:9d61b19d...`). Existing key files are never overwritten.\n\n3. **Define and publish a module** at A's prompt:\n   ```clojure\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
