{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > /private/tmp/jolt-p1c-audit.md <<'EOF'\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-19 17:09:43 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-20 00:09:43 ICT\nCoding-Agent: Codex (gpt-6.2-sol)\nSession-ID: 01a1f4c9-2302-78f0-b33d-90033750687a\nRole: VM Runtime Engineer (implementation)\n\n## P1 audit before implementation\n\nBase: \"'`88630634435b090f4b91e2897c8d1c23205e4c81` (`gambit-ffi-p1b`). References below use paths relative to either datomworld (`src/`) or Jolt (`host/`).\n\n- The product alias `deps.edn:95–97` invokes `yin.shell.main/-main`, which hands its storage composition to `yin.repl.main/main!` (`src/cljc/yin/shell/main.cljc:91–97`). The engine has its own `yin.repl.main/-main` calling `main!` with a nil composition (`src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc:1204–1257`). The engine entry is the appropriate local-memory slice. `startup` builds state, `boot` constructs the frontend catalog and attaches a driver (`:255–310`, `:588–681`), and `run-owner!` steps it, prints output and flushes prompts (`:1078–1103`). `repl/driver.cljc:172–179` dispatches local input to the Yin engine. `repl.cljc:1343–1350` uses the host reader; `:942–1008` constructs the actual Yin VM, expander, stream and index state. A loop over `jolt-compile-eval` alone would exercise Jolt, not these entry points or the Yin VM.\n- The Jolt owner needs epoch-clock, output/flush, exit, stdin/EOF and a wake seam, plus its host adapter. The current Jolt branch pumps stdin through `wake/sleep-fds!` and uses `System/currentTimeMillis` and `System/exit` (`main.cljc:1224–1250`). The Jolt host shadows the portable `yin.repl.host` and supplies native WebSocket connect/bind/unbind, pump and close rows (`src/jolt/yin/repl/host.jolt:16–34`). The Jolt waitset shadow is also native (`src/jolt/dao/stream/waitset/driver.jolt:163–212`). Gambit already boots the shared host table, reader, printer and compiler (`host/gambit/boot.ss:64–137`), owns real output ports (`host/gambit/rt-core.ss:604`), and exports clock, flush and stdin line/EOF (`host/gambit/host-vars.ss:37–58`). The native loader seam is missing: Gambit currently binds `require`/`use` to refusals (`host/gambit/host-vars.ss:407–408`) even though `host/chez/ns.ss:348–395` has a reusable source-loader hook. Full Jolt WebSocket/native waitset wiring is beyond the local-memory witness; frontends and APIs outside the qualified profile should refuse by capability.\n- `src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc:88–145` installs the hand-written forms-based Clojure frontend. The existing Jolt catalog omits PHP and installs Python only when its native C++ parser is available. For Gambit, only the core frontend is targeted here. The unavailable language diagnostic comes from frontend selection (`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:2342–2365`, `:2559–2600`). `src/dev/jolt_shell_session.clj:55–124` is an external subprocess harness for testing shell sessions, not an in-engine language frontend. Any in-session request for subprocesses must get the adapter'\"'s documented refusal; FASL and native compilation remain separate refusals (\"'`host/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:33–41`, `:852–881`). The gate'\"'s external watchdog can still launch gsi from the controlling shell.\n- \"'`jolt.ffi` is booted as native Jolt vars and compiler special forms (`host/gambit/boot.ss:129–137`, `host/gambit/host-ffi.ss`, `host/gambit/ffi/gambitffi-public.ss`). The Yin session has its own primitive/module registry (`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:812–871`, `:982–991`), so native Jolt namespace reachability alone does not expose any FFI to Yin programs. Any bridge must use an explicit Yin host primitive backed by the qualified Jolt API, with a visible result and error-path witness. Stage-1 qualification and C4 bounds govern everything beyond that witness.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
