ities\n     (dao.stream.codec/safe-number?), and decode's canonicality guard\n     rejects any non-shortest float anyway.\n\n   Decoding re-encodes and byte-compares, the same canonicality guard the\n   Boring and Dart halves apply: that one check rejects non-minimal heads,\n   indefinite lengths, wrong key order, duplicate map keys and set elements (compared by encoded bytes) and every non-canonical float.\n\n   Cross-host proof: the suites and fixtures of dao.stream.cbor-test and\n   the codec corpus, unchanged, over these bytes (no jolt-only corpus).\"\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n            [dao.host.float :as float]\n            [dao.host.text :as text]\n            [dao.stream.codec :as codec]))\n\n\n(def opts\n  \"The one encode option map the Boring halves use (kept for surface parity;\n   this implementation is always canonical, never stringref, never shaped).\"\n  {:profile :canonical :stringref false :shapes false})\n\n\n(def list-frame-tag\n  \"The symbol naming the list frame on both the encode and decode sides.\"\n  'dao.stream/list)\n\n\n(defn byte-payload?\n  \"True for the host byte payload written as a CBOR byte string (major 2):\n   jolt's byte array. The carrier dao.jing.stream's boundary adapter rides\n   Jing's canonical bytes on.\"\n  [x]\n  (bytes/bytes? x))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Byte vectors (ints 0-255) are the encoding medium; dao.host.bytes turns\n;; the finished vector into a fresh jolt byte array.\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n(defn- bcompare\n  \"Bytewise order, a proper prefix sorting first — Boring's canonical rule.\"\n  [a b]\n  (let [n (min (count a) (count b))]\n    (loop [i 0]\n      (if (= i n)\n        (- (count a) (count b))\n        (let [d (- (nth a i) (nth b i))]\n          (if (zero? d) (recur (inc i)) d))))))\n\n\n(defn- head-bytes\n  \"The minimal head